# Strings in zero-knowledge — the simplest case: a string equality, proven.
#
#   glass prove examples/prove/string_eq.glass        # -> result: 1, ACCEPT
#
# A string is not a field element, so "strings can't be proven" was the long-standing
# refusal. The cut: a string IS a value — a fixed-width sequence of codepoint wires,
# exactly the multi-wire layout ADTs and tuples already use. `++` is structural wire
# concatenation (no gate); `==` is the per-codepoint is-zero gadget, AND-folded into one
# 0/1 wire (different lengths => not equal, decided at circuit-build time). No new gate
# type, no verifier change — the existing Goldilocks gate model proves it.
#
# Here `"Hello, " ++ "World"` lowers to 12 codepoint wires and `==` compares them to the
# 12 wires of "Hello, World": equal => R = 1. (See private_prefix.glass / private_email.glass
# for the zero-knowledge payoff: the same machinery over a PRIVATE string input.)
("Hello, " ++ "World") == "Hello, World"
